# Judgment, Decision, and Reasoning Part II. S = questions in slide order, R = connection questions.
S = [
 ([2], "How do emotions take part in decision making?", "Emotions take part in decision making and do not only get in its way. People with damage to an area of their prefrontal cortex who have flattened emotions have impaired decision making."),
 ([2], "How does anxiety affect decisions?", "Anxious people tend to avoid decisions that could potentially lead to large negative consequences, which is risk avoidance, even when the option might turn out well."),
 ([2], "How does optimism affect decisions?", "Optimistic people are more likely to ignore negative information and focus on positive information, and too much optimism can lead to poor decisions."),
 ([3], "What are expected emotions?", "They are emotions that people predict they will feel concerning an outcome, and they guide which option the person is willing to take."),
 ([3], "How are expected emotions related to risk aversion?", "Expected emotions are one determinant of risk aversion, the tendency to avoid taking risks. A person who expects to feel very bad after a loss is less willing to accept a chance of it."),
 ([3], "How do people mispredict their emotions about losses and gains?", "People believe a particular loss will have a greater impact than a gain of the same size, which increases risk aversion."),
 ([4], "What was the procedure in Kermer and colleagues (2006)?", "Participants were given $5 and told that a coin flip would either win them another $5 or lose them $3, and they rated happiness beforehand as a baseline."),
 ([4], "What did participants expect in Kermer and colleagues (2006)?", "They expected losing $3 to lower happiness by about 4 points and winning $5 to raise it by about 2, so the expected loss was about twice the expected gain."),
 ([4], "What actually happened in Kermer and colleagues (2006), and why?", "The actual drop from losing was only about 1 point and the rise from winning about 1.2. People overestimate the negative feeling because coping mechanisms soften a loss, and they do not consider them when predicting."),
 ([5], "What are incidental emotions?", "They are emotions not specifically related to decision making, related to general disposition, a recent experience or the surroundings."),
 ([5], "What did Simonsohn (2007) find about admissions decisions?", "Applicants’ academic attributes were weighted more heavily on cloudy days than on sunny days, although the weather had nothing to do with the applicants."),
 ([5], "What did Simonsohn (2009) find about campus visits?", "Prospective students visiting a highly rated university were more likely to enroll if they had visited on a cloudy day."),
 ([6], "What was the procedure in Lerner and colleagues (2004)?", "Participants viewed a sad clip of a person dying, a disgust clip of a person using a dirty toilet, or a neutral clip of fish at the Great Barrier Reef, then set a price to sell and a price to buy a set of highlighters."),
 ([6], "What did Lerner and colleagues (2004) find for selling prices?", "Neutral asked about $4.50, disgust about $2.70 and sad about $3.00."),
 ([6], "What did Lerner and colleagues (2004) find for buying prices?", "Neutral offered about $3.50, disgust about $3.00 and sad about $4.50. Disgust lowered both prices, while sadness lowered the selling price and raised the buying price."),
 ([7], "How does the context of a decision change a physician’s choice?", "The cesarean test case was always the same. In the control context it was presented first, in the serious context it was preceded by four cases with serious complications, and in the not serious context it was preceded by four fairly routine cases. Significantly more doctors recommended cesarean after reading non-serious cases first."),
 ([8], "How do opt-in and opt-out procedures differ in organ donation consent?", "Under opt-in a person must take an active step to be a donor and consent is below 28 percent. Under opt-out a person is a donor unless they request not to be, and consent is over 99 percent."),
 ([8], "What is the status quo bias?", "It is the tendency to do nothing when faced with a difficult decision, so doing nothing leaves a person a non-donor under opt-in and a donor under opt-out."),
 ([8], "What choice does the short story The International Community present?", "A chicken, a duck, a turkey, a pheasant, a quail and a partridge were summoned and traveled together to the summit, where the king’s chef told them he had called them so they could tell him what sauce they would like to be eaten with. When one bird said it did not want to be eaten at all, the chef said that was out of the question, so the only choice offered was which sauce, which is a choice of the same kind as a default."),
 ([9], "What is the framing effect?", "It occurs when decisions are influenced by how a decision is stated, such as what might be gained or lost, while the facts of the choice stay the same."),
 ([9], "What did Tversky and Kahneman (1981) find about gain and loss framing?", "When situations are framed as gains, people tend toward a risk-aversion strategy and prefer the sure option. When framed as losses, they tend toward a risk-taking strategy and prefer the gamble."),
 ([9], "What are risk-aversion and risk-taking strategies?", "A risk-aversion strategy avoids an uncertain outcome in favor of a certain one, and a risk-taking strategy accepts an uncertain outcome in the hope of avoiding a certain loss."),
 ([11], "What are the programs in the gain frame of the disease problem in Tversky and Kahneman (1981)?", "In Tversky and Kahneman (1981), a disease is expected to kill 600 people. Program A saves 200 for certain, and Program B has a 1/3 probability that 600 are saved and a 2/3 probability that none are saved."),
 ([11], "What did people choose in the gain frame in Tversky and Kahneman (1981)?", "In Tversky and Kahneman (1981), 72 percent chose Program A, the sure option, and 28 percent chose Program B, which fits risk aversion."),
 ([12], "What are the programs in the loss frame of the disease problem in Tversky and Kahneman (1981)?", "In Tversky and Kahneman (1981), Program C means 400 people die for certain, and Program D has a 1/3 probability that no one dies and a 2/3 probability that 600 die."),
 ([12], "What did people choose in the loss frame in Tversky and Kahneman (1981), and why is it the framing effect?", "In Tversky and Kahneman (1981), 78 percent chose Program D and 22 percent chose Program C. Program C has the same outcome as A and Program D is the same gamble as B, so only the wording changed and most people switched to the gamble."),
 ([13], "What is neuroeconomics?", "It combines research from psychology, neuroscience and economics, and one finding is that decisions are influenced by emotions associated with activity in specific brain areas."),
 ([14], "How does the ultimatum game work in Sanfey and colleagues (2003)?", "A proposer gets $10 and offers a split to the responder, who can accept, so the money is divided as offered, or reject, so neither player receives anything."),
 ([14], "What is the rational decision for the responder according to utility theory?", "To accept any offer, because even $1 is worth more than the nothing that comes from rejecting."),
 ([15], "What were the offers and acceptance rates in Sanfey and colleagues (2003)?", "Participants played 20 games, 10 with humans and 10 with computers. $5–$5 and $7–$3 offers were accepted at about 100 and 95 percent for both partners, while $8–$2 was accepted about 53 percent from a human and 85 from a computer, and $9–$1 about 37 and 68 percent."),
 ([15], "Why were unfair offers rejected more often from humans in Sanfey and colleagues (2003)?", "In Sanfey and colleagues (2003), participants often rejected low offers because they became angry that the offers were unfair, and they were less angry with an unfair computer."),
 ([15], "What did brain activity show in Sanfey and colleagues (2003)?", "Participants with more activation of the right anterior insula, an area connected with emotional states, were more likely to reject more offers, which meant getting nothing."),
 ([16], "What is deductive reasoning?", "It is determining whether a conclusion logically follows from premises, which are the statements the reasoning starts from."),
 ([16], "What is a syllogism, and what is a categorical syllogism?", "A syllogism, introduced by Aristotle, has two premises and a conclusion. A categorical syllogism describes the relation between two categories using all, no or some."),
 ([16], "How does the birds-and-animals syllogism work?", "All birds are animals, all animals eat food, therefore all birds eat food. Birds fall inside animals and animals fall inside things that eat food."),
 ([17], "What does it mean for a syllogism to be valid?", "Its conclusion follows logically from its two premises, so if the premises of a valid syllogism are true then the conclusion must be true."),
 ([17], "How are validity and truth different?", "Validity depends on the form of the reasoning and truth on whether statements match the world. All birds are animals, all animals have four legs, all birds have four legs is valid but its conclusion is false because the second premise is false."),
 ([18], "How can a syllogism be invalid although every statement sounds reasonable?", "In All of the students are tired, some tired people are irritable, some of the students are irritable, every statement sounds reasonable but the conclusion does not follow from the premises."),
 ([18], "What is belief bias?", "It is the tendency to think a syllogism is valid if its conclusion is believable. 71 percent of participants judged invalid syllogisms with believable conclusions as valid."),
 ([19], "How can imagining the situation help solve reasoning problems?", "There is no easy method for judging whether a syllogism is valid, and one way is to picture the situation the statements describe. Most people solve the pool table problem by imagining how the balls are arranged."),
 ([19], "What is the answer to the pool table problem?", "The cue ball is to the left of the line of sight. With the red ball blocking the black ball, looking past it toward the black ball puts the cue ball off to the left."),
 ([20], "What is a mental model in Johnson-Laird (1999)?", "A mental model is a specific situation represented in a person’s mind that can be used to help determine the validity of syllogisms."),
 ([20], "What are the steps of the mental model approach?", "Create a model of the situation, generate tentative conclusions about it, look for exceptions to falsify the model, and determine validity from whether an exception was found."),
 ([20], "When is a conclusion valid in the mental model approach?", "A conclusion is valid only if it cannot be refuted by any model of the premises. If even one model fits the premises but not the conclusion, the conclusion does not follow."),
 ([21], "What is the form of a conditional syllogism?", "The first premise is of the form If p, then q, where p is a condition and q is what follows. The four types differ in the second premise, p, not p, q or not q."),
 ([21], "How many conditional syllogisms are valid?", "Two of the four are valid and two are not."),
 ([22], "What is modus ponens, and how often was it judged correctly?", "If p then q, p, therefore q, as in If I study I’ll get a good grade, I studied, therefore I’ll get a good grade. It is valid and 97 percent judged it correctly."),
 ([23], "What is modus tollens, and how often was it judged correctly?", "If p then q, not q, therefore not p, as in I didn’t get a good grade, therefore I didn’t study. It is valid, but only 60 percent judged it correctly."),
 ([24], "Why is conditional syllogism 3 not valid?", "If p then q, q, therefore p does not follow, because the first premise does not say studying is the only way to get a good grade. Only 40 percent judged it correctly."),
 ([24], "How does the same form with Tucson and Arizona show it?", "If I live in Tucson then I live in Arizona, I live in Arizona, therefore I live in Tucson fails, because a person can live in Arizona without living in Tucson."),
 ([25], "Why is conditional syllogism 4 not valid?", "If p then q, not p, therefore not q does not follow, because the first premise says nothing about what happens when someone does not study. Only 40 percent judged it correctly."),
 ([25], "How did people do across the four conditional syllogisms?", "The two valid ones were judged correctly at 97 and 60 percent, and the two invalid ones at 40 percent each, so both valid types were judged correctly more often."),
 ([26], "What is the Wason four-card problem?", "Four cards read E, K, 4 and 7, and the task is to find the minimum number to turn over to test the rule if there is a vowel on one side, then there is an even number on the other."),
 ([26], "Which cards should be turned over, and what do people choose?", "The E and the 7. 46 percent chose the 4, which is an error, and only 7 percent chose the 7, which is correct."),
 ([26], "Why is turning over the 7 a real test of the rule?", "A 7 with a vowel on its other side would break the rule, while a 4 cannot break the rule whatever is on its back."),
 ([27], "What is the falsification principle?", "To test a rule you must look for situations that falsify it, meaning prove it false. Most participants fail to do this."),
 ([27], "How does the concrete beer version change performance?", "With the rule If drinking beer, then 21 years old and cards Beer, Soda, 16 years old and 21 years old, 73 percent answered correctly. The correct cards are beer and 16 years old."),
 ([28], "What is a permission schema?", "It is the idea that if A is satisfied, B can be carried out, a familiar pattern of rule that grants permission once a condition is met."),
 ([28], "Why does a permission schema make the card problem easier?", "People are familiar with permission rules such as a drinking age, so a rule in a familiar setting is easier to reason about than an abstract one. Familiarity alone is not what matters."),
]
