 ("How do expected emotions and incidental emotions differ?", "Expected emotions are predictions about an outcome of the decision, while incidental emotions are not specifically related to the decision, such as weather or mood from an unrelated clip.", [3,5,6]),
 ("How do Simonsohn (2007, 2009) and Lerner and colleagues (2004) both show incidental emotions affect decisions?", "In Simonsohn (2007, 2009), weather changed how admissions attributes were weighted and enrollment, and in Lerner and colleagues (2004) mood from film clips changed buying and selling prices, though neither was related to the choice.", [5,6]),
