 ("How do expected emotions and the framing effect of Tversky and Kahneman (1981) each involve gains and losses?", "People expect a loss to have a greater impact than a gain of the same size, which increases risk aversion. In the framing effect, gain wording leads to the sure option and loss wording leads to the gamble, a risk-taking strategy that accepts uncertainty in the hope of avoiding a certain loss.", [3,9]),
 ("How does the ultimatum game of Sanfey and colleagues (2003) contrast utility theory with real behavior?", "Utility theory says accept any offer, but participants rejected low offers when angered by unfairness, giving up money.", [14,15]),
